2.1:
Olympian 2 For Theron of Acragas
Chariot Race 476 B. C. E.
Songs, rulers of the lyre, what god, what hero, what man shall we celebrate? Indeed, Pisa belongs to Zeus; and Heracles established the Olympic festival, as the finest trophy of battle;

6.1:
Pythian 6 For Xenocrates of Acragas
Chariot Race 490 B. C. E.
Listen! for we are again ploughing the field of dark-eyed Aphrodite, or of the Graces, as we approach the sacred navel of the loud-roaring land;

9.1:
Pythian 9 For Telesicrates of Cyrene
Hoplite Race 474 B. C. E.
With the help of the deep-waisted Graces I want to shout aloud proclaiming the Pythian victory with the bronze shield of Telesicrates, a prosperous man, the crowning glory of chariot-driving Cyrene;

5.1:
Isthmian 5 For Phylacidas of Aegina
Pancratium? 478 B. C. E.
Mother of the Sun, Theia of many names, for your sake men honor gold as more powerful than anything else;
